WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.280
In the world of J.R.R.

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.700
Tolkien, Middle-Earth itself is as much a
character as any of the story's heroes or

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:07.700
villains.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.419
Now, with the release of Amazon's upcoming
Lord of the Rings prequel, fans should expect

00:00:11.419 --> 00:00:13.960
to return to a few familiar locations.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.570
If there's one place that serves as a focal
point of the entire Lord of the Rings trilogy,

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.550
it has to be Mordor.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:25.380
This is where Sauron makes his home in Middle-Earth,
and it's where the One Ring was forged, too.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.990
As a result, Frodo's entire journey is undertaken
with the goal of reaching the Land of Shadow.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:34.370
From the story's beginning to its very end,
Mordor, Mount Doom, and Sauron's tower of

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:38.160
Barad-dûr are crucial to the plot.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.310
The good news for Tolkien fans is that these
are very important locations in the Second

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:43.449
Age, too.

00:00:43.449 --> 00:00:47.930
An appendix to The Return of the King explains
that, roughly 1,000 years into the Second

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:52.300
Age, Sauron chooses Mordor as a land to make
into a stronghold.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:57.320
"Its black gates are guarded by more than
just orcs.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.019
There is evil there that does not sleep."

00:01:01.019 --> 00:01:05.710
This is when he begins the building of Barad-dûr
— which isn't exactly an overnight project,

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:10.720
as it takes the Dark Lord and his servants
six whole centuries to complete construction.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.650
The very year Barad-dûr is finished, Sauron
enters the forge of Orodruin and creates his

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:16.780
One Ring.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:20.390
With all this in mind, it seems pretty much
definite that the Amazon series will return

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:24.740
to Mordor — and give fans the chance to
see the darkest corner of Middle-Earth in

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:25.740
its prime.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:29.940
The elves are far more involved in Middle-Earth's
politics during the Second Age than they are

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:30.940
in the Third.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:35.370
At this time, one of their most powerful kingdoms
is Lindon, located just to the west of what

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.400
will later be known as the Shire.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:42.280
Lindon is a vastly powerful kingdom that's
ruled by the elven high king, Gil-galad, whose

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.030
herald and right-hand man is a young elvish
noble named Elrond.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:50.430
By the time of The Lord of the Rings, just
a small remnant of Gil-galad's people remain

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.880
in Middle-Earth, having maintained a dwelling
at the elvish haven of Mithlond.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.549
Also known as the Grey Havens, this is where
Frodo and Bilbo reunite with Elrond, Galadriel,

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:03.810
and Gandalf, and set sail into the West at
the end of The Return of the King.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:08.229
As will be the case for many of the show's
locations, the Grey Havens of Amazon's series

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:11.370
will seem very different to how it's portrayed
in Lord of the Rings.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:16.440
Rather than appearing as an empty, faded stronghold
that quietly ferries elves across the sea,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:21.160
Mithlond will likely be a thriving crossroads
and safe haven for all the Free Peoples of

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:22.160
Middle-Earth.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.940
Elrond spends many of his early days serving
Gil-galad in Lindon.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.280
At one point, he leads an army out of the
elvish kingdom to battle the emergent forces

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.390
of Sauron.

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:35.570
When his host is defeated, Elrond retreats
northward to the edge of the Misty Mountains,

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:39.110
where he establishes a new stronghold called
Imladris.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:43.520
Also known as Rivendell, Elrond's homely home
attracts countless refugees of the ongoing

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.510
war against Sauron, and eventually becomes
a critical base of resistance against the

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.590
Dark Lord.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:54.370
Even to casual fans, Rivendell is one of the
most recognizable locations in all of Middle-Earth.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:58.540
Both The Hobbit and The Lord of the Rings
make multiple stops at the elvish refuge,

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:02.620
the most famous of which is the Council of
Elrond during The Fellowship of the Ring.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.260
In all of these cases, Elrond's domain is
depicted as a quiet and peaceful refuge from

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.850
the world's many perils.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.150
But things were very different during the
early days of Imladris.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:16.580
In fact, Elrond's fortress remained under
siege for years after it was first established

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.450
— painting quite a different picture than
the blissful oasis of calm that fans of the

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.290
movies have come to expect.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:26.070
No doubt the show's creators will leap at
the chance to bring the First Siege of Imladris

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:31.170
to the screen, giving fans a glimpse of this
familiar location in a very different age.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:36.210
Lothlorien is an ancient elvish realm, with
various woodland folk having lived there since

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.350
the earliest days of their history.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:42.370
Even as late as the Second Age, however, the
land looks very different to how it’s portrayed

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:43.370
in The Lord of the Rings.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:47.730
For one thing, Galadriel and her husband,
Celeborn, aren't in charge — at least, not

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:48.730
initially.

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:53.150
Partway through the Second Age, Galadriel
does arrive in Lothlorien, but even then as

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:55.010
a visitor and not a ruler.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.650
The huge, silver-barked and golden-leafed
Mallorn trees that define the look of the

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:03.060
Third Age's Lothlorien aren't there, either,
as Galadriel is the one who initially brings

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.950
the seeds for those mighty arboreal wonders.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:10.320
Nonetheless, Lothlorien plays an important
role in the events of the Second Age.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.750
The realm is ruled by the elvish leader named
Amdír, who fights — and dies — alongside

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.140
his kin during the War of the Last Alliance.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:21.910
So, while this version of Lothlorien will
likely have a very different vibe to Galadriel's

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:26.470
realm, you can probably make a safe bet that
it'll appear at least once in Amazon's upcoming

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:27.470
series.

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.190
The Fellowship's journey through the Mines
of Moria is one of the most famous sequences

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:32.370
in The Lord of the Rings.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:36.560
The nine travellers expect to meet Gimli's
cousin, Balin, but their hopes are quickly

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.840
dashed when they discover the dwarven kingdom
in ruins.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.880
Moria is also notorious for being home to
Durin's Bane, the monstrous Balrog that Gandalf

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.590
confronts on the Bridge of Khazad-dûm.

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:50.650
"You know what they awoke in the darkness
of Khazad-dûm.

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:56.820
Shadow and flame."

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:01.780
In the Second Age, however, there's no fiery
demon on the premises just yet, as the dwarven

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.090
kingdom of Khazad-dûm is still very much
in its heyday.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:10.070
As such, during the time of Amazon's series,
all those empty hallways and dusty tombs will

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:14.050
be well-lit, thriving, and positively filled
with dwarves.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.730
The Doors of Durin were also constructed during
the Second Age, as a kind of collaboration

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:23.280
between the dwarves of Khazad-dûm and the
elvish craftsmen of Eregion — chief among

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:26.770
whom is Celebrimbor, the elf-prince who forged
the Three Rings.

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:30.170
Fangorn Forest is a unique place in Middle-earth.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:34.220
Not only is it a dense, ancient wood, but
it's also the only known place where the Ents

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.290
still live during The Lord of the Rings.

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:40.610
During the Second Age, however, this sprawling
woodland is much larger — and may even connect

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:45.640
with the Old Forest, which lies directly adjacent
to the Shire, hundreds of miles away.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.350
In the books, Tolkien frequently hints that
the forests of Middle-Earth may once have

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:50.990
been far deeper than they currently appear.

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:55.640
In The Two Towers, for example, Treebeard
tells Merry and Pippin that, in the past:

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:00.060
"I could walk and sing all day and hear no
more than the echo of my own voice in the

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:01.060
hollow hills."

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.250
In addition, at the Council of Elrond, the
learned elf points out:

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:08.430
"Time was when a squirrel could go from tree
to tree from what is now the Shire to Dunland

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:09.840
west of Isengard."

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.060
In other words, Frodo's entire journey from
the Shire to the walls of Moria took place

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.420
over a land once filled with forest.

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:21.100
This is no big surprise, of course; Tolkien
was a huge lover of nature, and especially

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.100
of trees.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:26.180
As such, it seems like a fitting tribute to
the author himself to offer viewers a glimpse

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.200
into Tolkien's great forests at their absolute
greatest.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.900
Nestled at the northern edge of Mirkwood,
the Woodland Realm plays a central role in

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.280
the events of The Hobbit.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.510
King Thranduil captures Bilbo and his dwarvish
compatriots during their quest to the Lonely

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:44.780
Mountain, and later plays a crucial role in
the story's final battle.

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:49.340
But that isn't the first time that the woodland
elves become entangled in the affairs of Middle-earth.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:53.770
In fact, by the time of The Hobbit, the kingdom
has already existed in Mirkwood for thousands

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:56.480
of years, since the first half of the Second
Age.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.639
At this point, Mirkwood is known as the Greenwood,
and it's here that Thranduil's father, Oropher,

00:07:01.639 --> 00:07:03.300
decides to set up shop.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:07.640
Oropher's people ultimately get mixed up in
the events of the Last Alliance, and the elvish

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:10.080
king is slain during the Battle of Dagorlad.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.490
After this, Thranduil returns to the Greenwood
with the remnants of his army, constructs

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:19.020
the great Halls of Thranduil, and takes his
people underground for the next few thousand

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.020
years.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.290
Before Frodo, Sam, and Gollum enter Mordor,
they travel through a pass that's home to

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:28.870
Minas Morgul, the ghastly city inhabited by
the Nazgûl and their armies.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:33.370
The trio avoids the city by climbing a steep
mountain stair, battling through Shelob's

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:38.970
Lair, and finally arriving at Cirith Ungol,
another fortress built right into the mountainside.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:43.170
Both of these insidious strongholds are filled
with all kinds of evils — but they don't

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:44.480
start out that way.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:48.330
They're actually the work of the men of Númenor,
who build both fortresses to defend their

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:52.419
newly-founded kingdom of Gondor against the
forces of Sauron.

00:07:52.419 --> 00:07:56.970
While Cirith Ungol isn't constructed until
the Third Age, the city it guards is founded

00:07:56.970 --> 00:08:01.169
towards the end of the Second, easily fitting
into the show's timeline.

00:08:01.169 --> 00:08:06.370
Under Gondor's rule, Minas Morgul is known
as Minas Ithil, the Tower of the Moon, and

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:10.180
is supposed to act as a sister city to Minas
Tirith.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:14.730
Expect to see the construction of Minas Ithil
play a significant role in the later episodes

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:15.730
of Amazon's series.

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:20.480
Okay, let's rip this band-aid off quickly:
the truth is, there's little to no chance

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.550
that you're actually going to see the Shire
in the Amazon series — at least, not as

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:29.110
you know it — since the actual hobbit-filled
Shire doesn't appear on the map until well

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:30.680
into the Third Age.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.930
But that doesn't mean the land itself can't
make an appearance.

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:37.990
For one thing, the Shire lies near Gil-galad's
kingdom of Lindon, which is likely to feature

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:39.750
prominently in the show.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:44.560
Additionally, Galadriel and Celeborn temporarily
rule over a colony of elves in the region.

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.340
One way or another, there's a good chance
we'll get to see those rolling green hills

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:51.640
again, even if they don't have any halflings
living in them just yet.

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:56.170
The nearby community of Bree-land is more
likely to show up in the new show, though.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:09:00.930
This region is well-established by the Second
Age, and initially constitutes part of Gondor's

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:02.520
sister kingdom of Arnor.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.460
While it probably won't look quite the same,
Bree and its fellow townships could definitely

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:07.550
play a role in the story.

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:12.510
When Frodo, Sam, and Gollum journey through
the Dead Marshes, they encounter some creepy

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:15.630
aquatic ghouls that haunt the vast, mucky
quagmire.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:20.170
But who were these spirits, and just how did
they end up lost in that forsaken corner of

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:21.170
the world?

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:25.680
The answer lies in the Second Age — and,
in particular, the War of the Last Alliance.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.631
This epic confederation of elves, men, and
dwarves attacks Sauron in Mordor itself, but

00:09:30.631 --> 00:09:35.560
before they enter Mordor, they fight a huge
battle against Sauron's forces before the

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:36.560
gates of his domain.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:42.140
"A great battle, long ago… the Dead Marshes.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:47.672
Yes, yes, that is their name."

00:09:47.672 --> 00:09:48.672
In The Two Towers, Gollum explains:

00:09:48.672 --> 00:09:52.080
"They fought on the plain for days and months
at the Black Gates.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:57.560
But the Marshes have grown since then, swallowed
up the graves; always creeping, creeping."

00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:02.060
Amazon's prequel is almost guaranteed to show
this battle in all its macabre glory — albeit

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:03.870
probably not until a much later season.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:08.500
In The Lord of the Rings, the invasion of
Gondor begins with hordes of orcs invading

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.770
a ruined city called Osgiliath.

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:15.330
From there, they attack the capital city of
Gondor at the time — Minas Tirith.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:19.820
Except that, if you go back far enough, it's
actually Osgiliath that stands as the capital

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:20.820
of the nation.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:26.050
Minas Tirith, meanwhile, is simply a tower-fortress
built to help protect the nearby city.

00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:30.260
If Amazon's show depicts the fall of Númenor
and the establishment of Gondor — which

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:34.880
it almost certainly will — it's likely to
feature a much more impressive version of

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:36.550
the city of Osgiliath.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:40.800
From there, the chance to explore the surrounding
lands of Gondor, and the budding tower of

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:45.320
Minas Tirith, will be just too epic for Amazon
Studios to resist.

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:47.620
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:51.290
Plus, even more Looper videos about The Lord
of the Rings are coming soon.

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:55.380
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

